=== SURVEY ===

Reference slice: 0017 nl-search. nl-search retrieves over
drive_summary | charge_session | alert_history; nl-drive-search-replay
retrieves over drive_summary | route_segment | location_summary —
same SSE shape, same dispatcher loop, same withAiFeature/useAiEnabled
HOC, same off-mode invariant test pattern. The only structural
difference is that hydration here surfaces a `replayURL`
(=/drives/:id/replay) alongside the standard {title, subtitle,
url=/drives/:id, when} envelope so the user can jump directly to
the TripReplayPage.

Baseline non-AI behavior the slice MUST preserve:

  - web/src/features/driving/pages/DrivesListPage.tsx already
    renders the canonical typed-search + filter form for the
    /drives route: SearchInput (q=), FilterBar with score/length
    PillFilterBar chips, RangePicker (from=, to=), VehicleSelect
    (vehicle_id=), and ActiveFilterChips. The page reads from
    useDrives() (GET /api/v1/drives) and renders a table of
    Drive rows with click-through to /drives/:id and a
    /drives/:id/replay action. None of those surfaces is touched
    by this slice — they remain the canonical baseline.

  - TripReplayPage at /drives/:id/replay (the replay target) is
    likewise untouched by this slice.

  - GET /api/v1/drives + GET /api/v1/drives/{driveID}/telemetry
    remain the canonical backend baseline. This slice's new
    surface is entirely under POST /api/v1/ai/drives/search.

  - useSettings() carries ai_mode + ai_features.
    The slice neither flips ai_mode away from 'off' nor pre-enables
    the nl-drive-search-replay toggle; the registry entry sets
    DefaultOn=false so existing installations stay completely silent.

Registry route metadata (single source of truth):
  Backend:   POST /api/v1/ai/drives/search
  Frontend:  /drives
  UITestIDs: ai-feature-nl-drive-search-replay-root
  JobNames:  ai_drive_indexer
  PushKinds: []   (explicit empty, per the F1 contract)

=== REASONING ===

Why a new strategy package, not an inline branch:
  The dispatcher loop, decorator chain, redaction policy, and SSE
  envelope are identical to nl-search. Forking a sibling package
  (internal/ai/strategies/nl-drive-search-replay) gives this
  feature its own audit_feature label, its own goldens, and its
  own SystemPrompt — without leaking drive-specific load-bearing
  substrings into the nl-search strategy. The package follows
  the exact pattern set by slices 0017/0018/0019/0020.

Why a new HydratedDriveReplay envelope:
  HydratedResult (used by nl-search) carries {title, subtitle, url,
  when}. nl-drive-search-replay needs ONE extra field — replayURL —
  so the LLM can quote the user a single click-through to the
  TripReplayPage. Adding replayURL to HydratedResult would change
  every nl-search citation shape on disk (eval goldens) for no
  benefit, so the slice introduces a sibling DriveReplayHydrator
  port + HydratedDriveReplay envelope local to drive_search.go.
  The handler/hydrator pair (ai_drive_search_{handler,hydrator}.go)
  mirrors ai_search_{handler,hydrator}.go file-for-file.

Why source types route_segment + location_summary are package-
private constants today and NOT added to internal/ai/rag.Source*:
  internal/ai/rag/ is outside this slice's allowed-files block.
  The F7 PgvectorRetriever already accepts ANY $4 string in its
  SourceTypes filter — unknown types simply return zero rows.
  Reserving the two constants as package-private string literals
  in internal/ai/tools/drive_search.go lets retrieve_drive_chunks
  advertise the forward-compat allowlist without poisoning the
  F7 public API surface. A future slice that wires the actual
  route_segment / location_summary embeddings will promote the
  constants to rag.SourceRouteSegment / rag.SourceLocationSummary
  with no contract break.

Why the AI side panel renders ABOVE the typed filter bar, not below:
  The FilterBar carries primary controls (SearchInput, range
  picker, vehicle selector) that need to remain dominant in the
  visual hierarchy. Placing AINLDriveSearch above the FilterBar
  signals "this is an optional shortcut, the canonical typed
  controls are right below"; this is the same composition pattern
  slice 0017 used on the global SearchPage. The off-mode test
  proves the AI section is ABSENT — not greyed out — so off-mode
  users see exactly the same DrivesListPage they always have.

Why the canned mock-provider replies use single tool_call rounds:
  The basic_drive_query / replay_followup / zero_matches goldens
  call retrieve_drive_chunks once + hydrate_drive_replay once
  apiece, which keeps the dispatcher loop bounded at 2 tool
  rounds in the steady state. refusal_other_user calls neither
  tool — the strategy MUST refuse the request before any retrieval
  occurs (the user is trying to query another user's drives).
  All four goldens use canned mock-provider responses so the F6
  harness runs deterministically without real index data.

Why the indexer job is a fail-closed stub:
  The slice prompt lists ai_drive_indexer in the Off-mode contract
  impact section, so the registry MUST advertise it. The actual
  per-source re-embed loop (chunking, fan-out across drive_summary,
  route_segment, location_summary) is large enough to merit its
  own future slice. Until then, the gate stub at
  internal/jobs/ai_drive_indexer.go re-reads ai_mode + the
  per-feature toggle on every tick and returns Skipped=1 when off
  — proving the §I12 #3 contract today even though no fan-out
  happens yet. Direct sibling of internal/jobs/ai_docs_indexer.go
  from slice 0020.

Why the AI button is disabled and SSE wiring is deferred:
  The slice prompt's off-mode contract requires only the
  rendered-or-absent invariant. The full SSE call path lands
  alongside the indexer fan-out (which is what populates the
  drive_summary / route_segment / location_summary corpora). A
  half-wired button that fires against an empty index would
  return zero-match SSE streams to every user — confusing and
  baseline-violating. The disabled button preserves baseline
  intact and provides a concrete UI test ID for the off-mode
  test to assert against.

